cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata from this file."
title: "Membrane Arena: a browser game about three coupled membranes, reward schemes, a shield, an amplifier and a stabilizer (SYNTHETIC classical simulation)"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Brisson"
    given-names: "Jean-François"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0000-9778-5374"
    affiliation: "Spark AI NLP, Fredericton, New Brunswick, Canada"
version: 0.3.5
date-released: 2026-10-26
license: AGPL-3.0-only
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.23633126"
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: "10.5281/zenodo.23647863"
    description: "Version 0.3.4"
repository-code: "https://github.com/sparkainlp-x/membrane-arena"
abstract: >-
  SYNTHETIC educational game, zero dependencies, offline, English and French.
  The player steers three coupled Oja-rule membranes that learn a planted
  pattern in noise, chooses a reward scheme, a cooperation setting, a 2-of-3
  shield, a capped amplifier and a gain-clamped stabilizer, and watches a
  (1+1) evolution step. Scores are compared with the measured width-32 results
  of the crystallization-contest repository. Includes a daily seeded contest,
  a plain-text share card and challenge links. A Lab view plots the
  simulation's own instruments with confidence intervals, runs multi-seed
  ensembles and paired A/B tests against the repository baselines, and
  replays any contest. A brain page sizes layers of membranes with the
  2f + 1 rule and joins them with delayed links called microtubules (a
  metaphor only; nothing models real neurons, microtubules or a brain), and
  the Quorum Keeper logic game builds and breaks such fault-tolerant brains
  against an exhaustive adversary. Reverse Membranes runs the arena backwards
  at width 16: membranes with the learning sign flipped find a dark direction
  whose energy was removed ("opposite energy" is only the name of a score;
  nothing models negative energy, antimatter, physics or biology).
  Reverse Pair joins a reverse observer and a predictable calibrator that
  plays signed-amplitude sine tones on one clock, with an equalizer and a
  2-membrane shield, and ships a frozen evaluation protocol: thresholds
  tuned on calibration seeds, results on 120 held-out seeds with bootstrap
  intervals, on-grid and off-grid fades, baselines, an oracle and a
  regenerating script with SHA-256 hashes (nothing models oscillators or
  radio hardware). Pair Watch turns Reverse Pair into a visual game where
  the player is the second vote of the shield, with a logistic coach fitted
  on calibration runs and frozen, evaluated under its own frozen protocol on
  200 held-out runs, and replay codes checked by replaying the seed.
  Wave Pulse keeps the v0.3.4 locator and point estimate, and adds per-setting
  cost allowances for its nominal 90% region. These were fitted on 200 new
  calibration pulses and evaluated on 400 fresh held-out pulses per setting
  under a separate frozen protocol. The experimental result is mixed: 11 of
  16 per-setting Wilson intervals contain 90%, default coverage is 85.8%
  [82.0%, 88.8%], and one setting has no finite region after 20 of 200
  calibration pulses fell back. This does not establish 90% coverage across
  all settings; no real floor, pressure waves or holography are modeled.
  Classical simulation only.
